POTS Neuropathic Phenotype
Complex Fatigue Entities
Loss of peripheral vascular resistance

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-038
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe orthostatic intolerance, extreme leg discoloration, and near-syncope to rule out acute vascular occlusion or deep vein thrombosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 24-hour history of severe dizziness upon standing and bilateral legs turning dark purple and painful. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute bilateral deep vein thrombosis or impending circulatory collapse, and to stabilize hemodynamics before regular clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every time I stand, my legs turn completely purple and throb with pain, the room spins, and my vision goes black.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe acute exacerbation of known neuropathic POTS. Over the last 48 hours, they have experienced profound blood pooling in the lower extremities whenever upright. Symptoms include dependent acrocyanosis (legs turning dark purple/blue), intense aching in the calves, and extreme orthostatic tachycardia. The patient notes severe brain fog and near-syncope within 30 seconds of standing, severely restricting mobility to the point of being unable to access the kitchen.
Functional Status: Bedbound; reliant on crawling to access the bathroom to avoid syncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk secondary to cerebral hypoperfusion; unable to safely stand to prepare food or drink.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 supine, 90/60 standing mmHg | HR: 72 supine, 135 standing bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Pale when supine, visibly exhausted, coherent but speaking slowly.
Physical Exam: Postural vital signs reveal severe orthostatic tachycardia and a marked drop in systolic blood pressure upon standing. Lower extremities demonstrate profound dependent acrocyanosis that resolves instantly upon elevating the legs above the heart. Calves are soft, non-tender to deep palpation, with equal, palpable bilateral pedal pulses. Homan's sign is negative. Findings are classic for complete loss of peripheral vascular resistance.
Environmental Observations: Uneaten food in kitchen; patient unable to stand long enough to prepare a meal.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent changes. Adherent to current salt/fluid recommendations but overwhelmed by the severity of the flare.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: POTS Neuropathic Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical presentation, immediate resolution of cyanosis upon leg elevation, and absence of fixed, unilateral calf swelling or localized deep tenderness.

Cardiogenic Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal resting heart rate, regular rhythm, and strict positional dependence of the tachycardia.

Severe Clinical Dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Moist mucous membranes and normal skin turgor, indicating an issue of vascular distribution rather than absolute volume loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of severe dependent acrocyanosis and near-syncope required an urgent in-person assessment to definitively exclude bilateral vascular occlusion or critical hypovolemic shock. Upon confirming an acute exacerbation of peripheral neuropathic denervation (failure of leg veins to constrict), it was necessary to rapidly institute physical and dietary countermeasures. Leaving the patient unassessed would have inevitably resulted in a traumatic syncopal fall or an inappropriate ED transfer for a purely functional vascular pooling crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral rehydration salts directly on-site and instructed immediate application of waist-high, medical-grade compression tights. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Tadalafil (for endothelial support) and aggressive Fluid/Salt loading.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically putting on waist-high compression garments before your feet touch the floor in the morning, you will physically prevent blood from pooling in your legs, directly stabilizing your blood pressure and rewarding yourself with clear vision and safe standing.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Prescription for 30-40 mmHg waist-high compression garments provided. Shower chair strongly advised.
Follow-up: GP review in 3-5 days to consider the initiation of Midodrine or Fludrocortisone.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient actually loses consciousness, if a single leg becomes permanently pale, cold, and pulseless, or if chest pain develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The nerves controlling your leg veins are failing to signal them to squeeze when you stand; this requires aggressive mechanical and chemical management to overcome.
Clinical Course: Vascular pooling is highly distressing but resolves instantly when lying flat. Improvement requires strict adherence to compression and fluid expansion.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you actually pass out and hit the floor, or if one leg suddenly becomes completely white, freezing cold, and numb.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because the blood vessels in your legs aren't constricting properly, gravity is pulling all your blood away from your brain; the salt water physically expands your blood volume to compensate for the leaking vessels.
Actionable Steps: Drink 500ml of salty electrolyte fluid while lying flat before attempting to get out of bed tomorrow; this pre-loads your vascular system with fluid, directly preventing the extreme dizziness and reinforcing a safer morning routine.

